  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Cloud cover a real challenge for the overnight, \nand will have a big effect on temps and fog formation. \n\nSatellite obs and METARs show the few breaks of this aftn have \nclosed up. However, the clearing over Warren County is real and\nshould continue advancing eastward over the next few hours.\nHowever, there is a big discrepancy between guidance at T=0 and\nwhat's on the Sat images. Therefore, confidence is very low on\nthe speed and extent of clearing tonight. Have slowed down the\nclearing, mainly where the clouds are thickest and the RH\nguidance keeps the numbers highest later tonight (NE). Very\ntough forecast. MAV/MET MOS still paints a very cloudy and foggy\npicture at JST and fogginess at BFD later tonight. The northwest\nwind will get lighter soon, but the upslope into JST with\nlingering moisture is usually a strong sign of poor visby/low\ncigs there. Have kept a mention of patchy fog almost everywhere\novernight. But, the extent and thickness of the fog is highly\ndebatable. Doesn't hurt to mention it, but this part of the \nforecast is usually more impactful to aviation than the general \nwx grids/forecasts.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Noticeable warmup and several days of dry \nconditions to end September and start October\n\nA warmup will last through the mid to late week period with \nfcst max temps in the 75-85F range or +10-15 degrees above the \nhistorical normal for the end of September/beginning of October.\n\nFor context, the average last date of max temp >=80F is 9/27 at\nState College, 9/29 at Altoona, 10/2 at Williamsport and 10/5 \nat Harrisburg. \n\nA small chance for --RA/SHRA is here for later Wed night and\nThurs AM as a weak warm front aloft (mainly an increase in\nmoisture) passes overhead. The moisture plume will probably be\nthick enough to drop the clouds low enough for a little light\nrain to hit the ground over the nrn half of the CWA. The highest\nchances will be in the far NW. But, some sprinkles could get\ndown just south of I-80 around sunrise Thurs.\n\nShowers are again possible on Friday-Friday night associated \nwith cold front sinking southeastward from the eastern Great \nLakes. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n \nKEY MESSAGE 3: Cooling trend this weekend into early next week \nwith an increasing chance of rain Saturday and Sunday. \n\n24hr maxT change on Saturday behind the cold front is fcst to \nbe 10-15 degrees lower vs. Friday with highs in the low 60s/70s.\nTrailing frontal wave could bring more widespread soaking rain\non Sunday based on WPC/NBM QPF - although EC/CMC and (to lesser\nextent) lean farther to the south/east with the frontal precip.\nConfidence is higher that temps continue to cool off and drop \nnear or below the historical average into early next week. Frost\npotential is on the table for next Monday and Tuesday night.",
